{"book":"Jeremiah","chapter":22,"evidence_availability":"AVAILABLE","failure_reason":null,"generated_metadata":{"commentary_prompt_version":"1.8","commentary_schema_version":"1.2","evidence_bundle_version":"1.1","evidence_hash":"098b8df90d9eb8b179a4cc9bf42aeabb17ee69997004ea35233aba85689cf182","generated_timestamp":null,"model":"gpt-5.6-terra","renderer_label":"commentary-v1.2-codex-session","synthesis_compiler_version":"1.1","synthesis_hash":"2eea59747efaf56804ad7eebabc6bf053462a7378b383b8eb8a4d46e22b6ddd1","synthesis_schema_version":"1.1"},"reference":"Jeremiah 22","sections":[{"blocks":[{"confidence":"medium","evidence_ids":["haggai-jeremiah-signet","haggai-zerubbabel"],"id":"block_1","interpretation_level":"disputed","synthesis_ids":["syn_interpretive_questions_09f80eee5e70"],"text":"The removal of Coniah as a signet in verses 24–30 is later set in striking contrast with Haggai’s description of Zerubbabel as God’s chosen servant and signet, an image carrying royal-Davidic significance without saying that Zerubbabel became king. The shared image makes the contrast worth noticing, but it cannot by itself prove that Haggai deliberately reversed Jeremiah’s oracle.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 22:24-30"]}],"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Later Signet Image"},{"blocks":[{"confidence":"medium","evidence_ids":["jeremiah-long-composition","jeremiah-practical-mt-outline"],"id":"block_2","interpretation_level":"disputed","synthesis_ids":["syn_surrounding_passages_26feec2cf420"],"text":"Jeremiah 22 appears within a received book that brings together poetry, prose sermons, narratives, letters, headings, repeated accounts, restoration collections, oracles concerning nations, and an appendix. This broad outline describes the book’s present form without deciding how its long compositional and textual history developed.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 10:1-25","Jeremiah 11:1-23","Jeremiah 12:1-17","Jeremiah 13:1-27","Jeremiah 14:1-22","Jeremiah 15:1-21","Jeremiah 16:1-21","Jerem